Reason:
The first statement returns `null` when the end of the input is reached without cleaning up the resources, which leads to potential resource leaks. The second statement, when not executed, fails to close the `reader`, thereby leaving resources open and improperly managing the state of the `LineEntityProcessor`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
if (line == null) {
    closeResources();
    return null;
}
```
```java
public void closeResources() {
    if (reader != null) {
        IOUtils.closeQuietly(reader);
    }
    reader = null;
}
```
Reason:
The patch introduces a call to `closeResources()` when the end of input is detected, ensuring that the `reader` is properly closed and the resources are cleaned up. The `closeResources()` method abstracts the cleanup logic, preventing resource leaks and ensuring proper state management within the `LineEntityProcessor`.

